row @17,102,694,150
  col @17,102,682,150
    h1 ARIA Authoring Practices Guide (APG) Home @17,102,682,25
    text Learn to use the accessibility semantics defined by the Accessible Rich Internet Application (ARIA) specification to create accessible web experiences. This guide describes how to apply accessibility semantics to common design patterns and widgets. It provides design patterns and functional examples complemented by in-depth guidance for fundamental practices. @17,139,682,81
    link "View Patterns" patterns/ @17,232,682,20
  image https://www.w3.org/WAI/content-images/wai-aria-practices/images/index-1.svg "A laptop screen fills with an accessibility icon and emits a checkmark." @711,102,0,0
h2 APG Resources @17,264,694,22
text Building blocks that help you make the web accessible @17,298,694,20
row @17,330,694,124
  col @17,330,682,124
    h3 Design Patterns and Examples @17,330,682,19
    text Learn how to make accessible web components and widgets with ARIA roles, states and properties and by implementing keyboard support. One or more ways of implementing each pattern is demonstrated with a functional example. @17,361,682,61
    link "Learn more about APG patterns and examples" patterns/ @17,434,682,20
  image https://www.w3.org/WAI/content-images/wai-aria-practices/images/index-2.svg "A menagerie of widgets." @711,330,0,0
row @17,466,694,104
  col @17,466,682,104
    h3 Use ARIA Landmarks @17,466,682,19
    text Learn how to use HTML sectioning elements and ARIA landmark roles to make it easy for assistive technology users to understand the meaning of the layout of a page. @17,497,682,40
    link "Learn more about ARIA landmarks" practices/landmark-regions/ @17,549,682,21
  image https://www.w3.org/WAI/content-images/wai-aria-practices/images/index-3.svg "A document flies apart into chunks." @711,466,0,0
row @17,582,694,103
  col @17,582,682,103
    h3 Providing Accessible Names and Descriptions @17,582,682,18
    text Providing elements with accessible names and, where appropriate, accessible descriptions is one of the most important responsibilities authors have when developing accessible web experiences. @17,612,682,41
    link "Learn more about accessible names and descriptions" practices/names-and-descriptions/ @17,665,682,20
  image https://www.w3.org/WAI/content-images/wai-aria-practices/images/index-4.svg "Indicators delve inside a document." @711,582,0,0
row @17,697,694,104
  col @17,697,682,104
    h3 And So Much More... @17,697,682,19
    text Learn about other fundamental practices related to correctly using accessibility semantics, developing keyboard interfaces, and more. @17,728,682,41
    link "Learn more about APG practices" practices/ @17,781,682,20
  image https://www.w3.org/WAI/content-images/wai-aria-practices/images/index-5.svg "A box with an accessibility label is chock full of widgets and document bits." @711,697,0,0
h2 Get Involved @17,813,694,21
text The APG Task Force relies on broad community representation and participation to continuously improve the usefulness and quality of the APG. There are a variety of ways you can get involved and help promote development of accessible experiences. @17,846,694,61
row @17,919,694,358
  col @17,919,137,358
    image https://www.w3.org/WAI/content-images/wai-aria-practices/images/index-6.svg "An icon showing three nodes connecting." @17,919,0,0
    h3 Join the Task Force @17,931,137,38
    text To join the APG Task Force, individuals need to first join the W3C ARIA Working Group. Participants are expected to actively contribute to the work of the task force. @17,981,137,203
    link "Learn more about the work of the task force and how to join" https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/task-forces/practices/ @17,1196,137,81
  col @166,959,246,278
    image https://www.w3.org/WAI/content-images/wai-aria-practices/images/index-7.svg "An icon showing two human shapes carrying a burden." @166,959,0,0
    h3 Contribute via GitHub @166,971,246,19
    text Many valuable contributions are made by people who find or raise issues of interest in our GitHub repository and then submit proposed changes via a GitHub pull request. If you choose this path, please start by studying our guidelines for contributing to the repository and maintaining code quality. @166,1002,246,182
    link "View ReadMe in the GitHub repository" https://github.com/w3c/aria-practices @166,1196,246,41
  row @424,955,287,286
    image https://www.w3.org/WAI/content-images/wai-aria-practices/images/index-8.svg "A notification bell icon appears over an email icon." @424,955,0,0
    col @436,955,275,286
      h3 Mailing Lists @436,955,275,18
      text The APG Task Force uses the public aria-practices mailing list for email discussion. Meeting announcements, agendas, and links to minutes are sent to the mailing list. While GitHub issues are the preferred place to discuss APG content, the mailing list is available to anyone who would prefer to communicate with the APG Task Force via email. @436,985,275,203
      link "View the aria-practices mailing list archive" https://lists.w3.org/Archives/Public/public-aria-practices/ @436,1200,275,41
